Description:DETAILED DESCRIPTION

[00023] The following detailed description illustrates embodiments of the present disclosure and ways in which they can be implemented. Although some modes of carrying out the present disclosure have been disclosed, those skilled in the art would recognise that other embodiments for carrying out or practising the present disclosure are also possible.

[00024] The description set forth below in connection with the appended drawings is intended as a description of certain embodiments of bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing and is not intended to represent the only forms that may be developed or utilised. The description sets forth the various structures and/or functions in connection with the illustrated embodiments; however, it is to be understood that the disclosed embodiments are merely exemplary of the disclosure that may be embodied in various and alternative forms. The figures are not necessarily to scale; some features may be exaggerated or minimised to show details of particular components. Therefore, specific structural and functional details disclosed herein are not to be interpreted as limiting, but merely as a representative basis for teaching one skilled in the art to variously employ the present invention.

[00025] While the disclosure is susceptible to various modifications and alternative forms, specific embodiment thereof has been shown by way of example in the drawings and will be described in detail below. It should be understood, however, that it is not intended to limit the disclosure to the particular forms disclosed, but on the contrary, the disclosure is to cover all modifications, equivalents, and alternatives falling within the scope of the disclosure.

[00026] The terms “comprises”, “comprising”, “include(s)”, or any other variations thereof, are intended to cover a non-exclusive inclusion, such that a setup, or system that comprises a list of components or steps does not include only those components or steps but may include other components or steps not expressly listed or inherent to such setup or system. In other words, one or more elements in a system or apparatus preceded by “comprises... a” does not, without more constraints, preclude the existence of other elements or additional elements in the system or apparatus.

[00027] In the following detailed description of the embodiments of the disclosure, reference is made to the accompanying drawings and which are shown by way of illustration-specific embodiments in which the disclosure may be practiced. These embodiments are described in sufficient detail to enable those skilled in the art to practice the disclosure, and it is to be understood that other embodiments may be utilized and that changes may be made without departing from the scope of the present disclosure. The following description is, therefore, not to be taken in a limiting sense.

[00028] The present disclosure will be described herein below with reference to the accompanying drawings. In the following description, well-known functions or constructions are not described in detail since they would obscure the description with unnecessary detail.

[00029] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is disclosed, in accordance with one embodiment of the present invention. It comprises of cellulose-based hydrogel matrix 102, miniaturized microchips 104, ph sensor 106, temperature sensor 108, wireless communication module 110, biocompatible power source 112, mobile application 114 and alert system 116.

[00030] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, the present disclosure provides details of bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100. It is a smart wound dressing designed to enable real-time monitoring of healing progress and potential complications through embedded sensing and communication technologies. The invention incorporates a cellulose-based hydrogel matrix 102, which retains moisture and provides biocompatibility, and miniaturized microchips 104 for seamless integration within the dressing. In one of the embodiments, the system includes key components such as ph sensor 106 and temperature sensor 108, enabling the detection of critical wound biomarkers. The wireless communication module 110 transmits real-time data to a mobile application 114, facilitating remote monitoring and timely intervention. The biocompatible power source 112 ensures continuous operation, while the alert system 116 notifies healthcare providers of abnormal conditions. These components collectively create a novel system to improve wound care outcomes by enhancing monitoring efficiency, patient comfort, and early complication detection. 

[00031] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with cellulose-based hydrogel matrix 102, which forms the foundational layer of the dressing. It retains moisture to create an optimal healing environment and ensures biocompatibility for safe application on wounds. The hydrogel matrix 102 securely embeds miniaturized microchips 104 during the gelation process, maintaining stability and flexibility. It also provides a protective barrier for the embedded sensors, preventing external contamination and enhancing durability.

[00032] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100is provided with miniaturized microchips 104, which are seamlessly integrated into the hydrogel matrix 102. These microchips serve as the core processing units, supporting the functionality of the ph sensor 106 and temperature sensor 108. The microchips 104 are equipped with embedded wireless communication module 110 to transmit data and work in tandem with the biocompatible power source 112 to ensure uninterrupted operation. Their small size and flexibility make them ideal for integration without affecting the dressing's comfort and usability.

[00033] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with ph sensor 106, which detects changes in the pH level of the wound environment. This sensor plays a critical role in identifying infection risks by monitoring acidity or alkalinity fluctuations. The ph sensor 106 continuously sends data to the miniaturized microchips 104, which process the information and transmit it via the wireless communication module 110 to the mobile application 114 for real-time monitoring by healthcare providers.

[00034] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with temperature sensor 108, which tracks variations in wound temperature to identify potential signs of infection or inflammation. The temperature sensor 108 works in conjunction with the ph sensor 106 to provide comprehensive biomarker data. This sensor relays temperature readings to the miniaturized microchips 104, which analyze and transmit the data wirelessly to the mobile application 114 for healthcare analysis.

[00035] Referring to Fig. 1, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with wireless communication module 110, which enables seamless data transmission from the miniaturized microchips 104 to the mobile application 114. This module ensures that real-time wound condition data from the ph sensor 106 and temperature sensor 108 is available to healthcare providers remotely. The wireless communication module 110 minimizes the need for manual inspections, contributing to patient comfort and effective wound management.

[00036] Referring to Fig. 1, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with biocompatible power source 112, which powers the miniaturized microchips 104 and the sensors embedded within the hydrogel matrix 102. This power source is designed to operate continuously, utilizing either small biocompatible batteries or energy-harvesting techniques. It ensures that the wireless communication module 110 functions efficiently, providing uninterrupted data transmission to the mobile application 114.

[00037] Referring to Fig. 1 and Fig. 2, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with mobile application 114, which serves as the interface for real-time data visualization and monitoring. The application receives information from the wireless communication module 110, processes it, and displays actionable insights for healthcare providers. It also integrates an alert system 116 to notify users of critical biomarker thresholds, enabling timely medical intervention.

[00038] Referring to Fig. 1, bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100 is provided with alert system 116, which is integrated into the mobile application 114. The alert system 116 triggers notifications if data from the ph sensor 106 or temperature sensor 108 indicates abnormal conditions such as infection risks. This system ensures prompt attention from healthcare providers, reducing complications and enhancing wound care outcomes.

[00039] Referring to Fig 3, there is illustrated method 200 for bio-responsive wound dressing with integrated microchip sensing 100. The method comprises:  
At step 202, method 200 includes preparing the cellulose-based hydrogel matrix 102 by integrating miniaturized microchips 104 during the gelation process to ensure secure and seamless placement;
At step 204, method 200 includes functionalizing the ph sensor 106 and temperature sensor 108 embedded in the hydrogel matrix 102 to enable the detection of wound biomarkers;
At step 206, method 200 includes the sensors 106 and 108 continuously monitoring critical wound conditions, such as pH levels and temperature, to detect healing progress or signs of infection;
At step 208, method 200 includes transmitting real-time data from the miniaturized microchips 104 via the wireless communication module 110 to the mobile application 114;
At step 210, method 200 includes the mobile application 114 displaying the wound data, including pH and temperature trends, for healthcare providers to monitor remotely;
At step 212, method 200 includes the biocompatible power source 112 providing continuous energy to the sensors 106 and 108, the miniaturized microchips 104, and the wireless communication module 110 to ensure uninterrupted functionality;
At step 214, method 200 includes the alert system 116 integrated with the mobile application 114 triggering notifications when biomarker readings from the sensors 106 and 108 exceed critical thresholds, enabling timely medical intervention.
[00040] The invention finds applications in advanced wound care where continuous monitoring is critical for effective healing. The cellulose-based hydrogel matrix 102 provides a moist, biocompatible environment, promoting faster healing while reducing the risk of infection. The integration of miniaturized microchips 104, ph sensor 106, and temperature sensor 108 enables real-time tracking of key biomarkers, allowing healthcare providers to monitor wound conditions remotely. The wireless communication module 110 transmits data seamlessly to the mobile application 114, which displays actionable insights and triggers notifications through the alert system 116 for timely medical intervention. Powered by a biocompatible power source 112, the invention ensures uninterrupted operation without frequent maintenance. This innovation enhances patient comfort by minimizing dressing changes, reduces healthcare costs through remote monitoring, and enables early detection of complications, leading to improved treatment outcomes and overall efficiency in wound care management.

